{"id": 114527, "acmi_id": "B2003245", "title": "[Royal Visit, Victoria, 1954: Pre-Release Footage: Reel 21]", "title_annotation": "", "slug": "royal-visit-victoria-1954-pre-release-footage-reel-21", "creator_credit": "State Film Centre of Victoria", "credit_line": "", "headline_credit": "Australia, 1954", "record_type": "work", "type": "Film", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "external": false, "first_production_date": "1954-01-01T10:00:00+10:00", "brief_description": "<p>The 1954 visit to Australia of newly-crowned Queen Elizabeth II with her husband and consort Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, was a cause of much celebration - and an occasion for much pageantry - in Victoria. Elizabeth was the first reigning monarch to visit Australia, and the young and popular royal couple attracted intense interest wherever they visited on their extensive Australian tour.</p>\n<p>This silent, pre-release footage - much of which eventually comprised sections of the official Victorian film release - follows the visiting couple on their journey throughout the state; including numerous regional centres as well as major sites and events in Melbourne. It shows not only the &#8220;official&#8221; portrait of this historic royal visit, but also dwells on the reactions of the Victorian people; crowds of whom waited by roadsides and railways, on city streets and in small country towns, for a glimpse of the monarch.</p>\n<p>As well as focusing on the history of the 1954 royal visit, the footage thus also gives us a unique and invaluable look back in time at some of the buildings, streetscapes, vehicles, cameras, fashions and crowd behaviours of the mid-1950s in Victoria.</p>\n<p>Reel 21 contains further footage of the visit to Mildura (also seen in Reel 20) and nearby Red Cliffs.</p>\n<p>Detailed Description Reel 21:</p>\n<p>Opens with continuation of footage from previous Reel 20 (B2003244), of the Queen and Duke visiting a private property to observe the grape harvest. 00:00:17:0 Title &#8220;Redcliffs Quondong Park&#8221; (sic) followed by footage of children in the crowd waving flags, and the Queen and Duke on a dais, where the Duke makes a speech. 00:01:12:00 Queen and Duke leave dais and get into an open jeep for a drive through the crowd. 00:02:04:00 Queen and Duke leave the Park in a regular street car. 00:02:16:00 Mildura airfield, Queen and Duke farewell officials and citizens before boarding plane and departing.</p>", "constellations_primary": [], "constellations_other": [], "recommendations": [], "title_for_label": "<p>[Royal Visit, Victoria, 1954: Pre-Release Footage: Reel 21]</p>", "creator_credit_for_label": "<p>State Film Centre of Victoria</p>", "headline_credit_for_label": "<p>Australia, 1954</p>", "description": "<p>The 1954 visit to Australia of newly-crowned Queen Elizabeth II with her husband and consort Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, was a cause of much celebration - and an occasion for much pageantry - in Victoria. Elizabeth was the first reigning monarch to visit Australia, and the young and popular royal couple attracted intense interest wherever they visited on their extensive Australian tour.</p>\n<p>This silent, pre-release footage - much of which eventually comprised sections of the official Victorian film release - follows the visiting couple on their journey throughout the state; including numerous regional centres as well as major sites and events in Melbourne. It shows not only the &#8220;official&#8221; portrait of this historic royal visit, but also dwells on the reactions of the Victorian people; crowds of whom waited by roadsides and railways, on city streets and in small country towns, for a glimpse of the monarch.</p>\n<p>As well as focusing on the history of the 1954 royal visit, the footage thus also gives us a unique and invaluable look back in time at some of the buildings, streetscapes, vehicles, cameras, fashions and crowd behaviours of the mid-1950s in Victoria.</p>\n<p>Reel 21 contains further footage of the visit to Mildura (also seen in Reel 20) and nearby Red Cliffs.</p>\n<p>Detailed Description Reel 21:</p>\n<p>Opens with continuation of footage from previous Reel 20 (B2003244), of the Queen and Duke visiting a private property to observe the grape harvest. 00:00:17:0 Title &#8220;Redcliffs Quondong Park&#8221; (sic) followed by footage of children in the crowd waving flags, and the Queen and Duke on a dais, where the Duke makes a speech. 00:01:12:00 Queen and Duke leave dais and get into an open jeep for a drive through the crowd. 00:02:04:00 Queen and Duke leave the Park in a regular street car. 00:02:16:00 Mildura airfield, Queen and Duke farewell officials and citizens before boarding plane and departing.</p>", "description_for_label": ["<p>The 1954 visit to Australia of newly-crowned Queen Elizabeth II with her husband and consort Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, was a cause of much celebration - and an occasion for much pageantry - in Victoria. Elizabeth was the first reigning monarch to visit Australia, and the young and popular royal couple attracted intense interest wherever they visited on their extensive Australian tour.</p>\n<p>This silent, pre-release footage - much of which eventually comprised sections of the official Victorian film release - follows the visiting couple on their journey throughout the state; including numerous regional centres as well as major sites and events in Melbourne. It shows not only the &#8220;official&#8221; portrait of this historic royal visit, but also dwells on the reactions of the Victorian people; crowds of whom waited by roadsides and railways, on city streets and in small country towns, for a glimpse of the monarch.</p>\n<p>As well as focusing on the history of the 1954 royal visit, the footage thus also gives us a unique and invaluable look back in time at some of the buildings, streetscapes, vehicles, cameras, fashions and crowd behaviours of the mid-1950s in Victoria.</p>\n<p>Reel 21 contains further footage of the visit to Mildura (also seen in Reel 20) and nearby Red Cliffs.</p>\n<p>Detailed Description Reel 21:</p>\n<p>Opens with continuation of footage from previous Reel 20 (B2003244), of the Queen and Duke visiting a private property to observe the grape harvest. 00:00:17:0 Title &#8220;Redcliffs Quondong Park&#8221; (sic) followed by footage of children in the crowd waving flags, and the Queen and Duke on a dais, where the Duke makes a speech. 00:01:12:00 Queen and Duke leave dais and get into an open jeep for a drive through the crowd. 00:02:04:00 Queen and Duke leave the Park in a regular street car. 00:02:16:00 Mildura airfield, Queen and Duke farewell officials and citizens before boarding plane and departing.</p>"], "credit_line_for_label": "", "details": [{"label": "Duration", "display_values": ["00:03:52:00"]}, {"label": "Subject categories", "display_values": ["Anthropology, Ethnology, Exploration & Travel&nbsp;→ Mildura (Vic.)", "Anthropology, Ethnology, Exploration & Travel&nbsp;→ Red Cliffs (Vic.)", "Anthropology, Ethnology, Exploration & Travel&nbsp;→ Victoria - Description and travel", "Archival, Cinemagazines & Newsreels&nbsp;→ Archival materials", "Economics, Philosophy, Politics, Religion & Sociology&nbsp;→ Visits of state", "History&nbsp;→ Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain, 1926-", "History&nbsp;→ Philip, Prince, consort of Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain, 1921-", "History&nbsp;→ Victoria - History", "History&nbsp;→ Visits of state", "People&nbsp;→ Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain, 1926-", "People&nbsp;→ Philip, Prince, consort of Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain, 1921-", "Places&nbsp;→ Mildura (Vic.)", "Places&nbsp;→ Red Cliffs (Vic.)", "Places&nbsp;→ Victoria", "Short films", "Short films&nbsp;→ Short films - Australia"]}, {"label": "Sound/audio", "display_values": ["Silent"]}, {"label": "Col